.TH YOM 1 "2026-08-01" "v2.3.0" "YOM User Manual"
.SH NAME
yom - lightweight, non-interactive micro-shell and systems glue
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B yom
[\fISCRIPT_FILE\fR]
.br
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B yom
is a lightweight, high-performance micro-shell and systems glue utility
written in Rust. Developed as a memory-safe alternative to \fBdash\fR,
it provides efficient scripting execution while avoiding the compilation
dependencies associated with traditional C-based shells.
.PP
When invoked with a \fISCRIPT_FILE\fR, \fByom\fR parses and executes the contained
commands sequentially. If no arguments are provided, the utility terminates
silently with exit code 0.
.PP
Because \fByom\fR compiles into a single, static binary, it is suitable for deployment
as a drop-in execution processor (within limits) in minimal Unix environments, containers,
and custom \fBinitramfs\fR images.
.SH SYNTAX
.B yom
employs a POSIX-inspired syntax style. To execute a command synchronously
and wait for its completion, specify the absolute path:
.RS
.EX
/bin/program
.EE
.RE
.PP
To execute a command asynchronously in the background, append an ampersand:
.RS
.EX
/bin/program &
.EE
.RE
.PP
To replace the current shell process image with the target program, use the
.B exec
builtin:
.RS
.EX
exec /bin/program
.EE
.RE
.PP
.B yom
implements the following standard shell builtins:
.TP
.B echo \fR[\fItext\fR]
Prints text to standard output.
.TP
.B read \fR[\fIprompt\fR]
Displays a prompt and reads input from standard input.
.TP
.B cd \fR[\fIpath\fR]
Changes the current working directory.
.TP
.B exit \fR[\fIcode\fR]
Terminates the shell process with the specified status code.
.TP
.B pwd
Prints the absolute path of the current working directory.
.TP
.B export \fR[\fIname\fR=\fIvalue\fR]
Sets an environment variable for child processes and subsequent executions.
.PP
Environment variables are referenced by prefixing the variable name with a dollar sign (\fB$\fR):
.RS
.EX
export ls=/bin/ls
$ls
$ls &
exec $ls
.EE
.RE
.PP
The
.B set
builtin modifies shell execution options. Use a minus sign (\fB-\fR) to enable an option and a plus sign (\fB+\fR) to disable it:
.RS
.EX
set -e
.EE
.RE
Enables immediate termination if a command exits with a non-zero status.
.RS
.EX
set +e
.EE
.RE
Disables immediate termination on command failure.
.PP
Additionally, \fByom\fR includes a non-POSIX builtin named \fIhook\fR, which redirects standard builtins to external binaries or extends shell functionality:
.RS
.EX
hook <hooked> </path/to/hook/>
.EE
.RE
For comprehensive details, refer to the
.BR yom-hooks(3)
manual page.
.SS If Statements
Conditional evaluation uses standard POSIX-style syntax:
.RS
.EX
if [ 1 -eq 1 ]
then
  echo "one is equal to one"
fi
.EE
.RE
.PP
Variables defined via \fBexport\fR may also be evaluated within conditional blocks, provided they are prefixed with \fB$\fR to ensure value comparison rather than literal string comparison.
.SH NOTES
If you want to pass
.B \&
as an argument, surround it in quotes like this:
.RS
.EX
"&"
.EE
.RE
then you can run it with
.B \&
as an argument.

.SS Neat Stuff
in
.B yom
if you don't surround the operands with quotes and they arn't integers
then
.B yom
will treat them as strings, pretty cool.
.br
Another neat thing,
.B [
isn't a alias for the
.B test
command, it is a bit of syntax to emulate the look of POSIX, so that
.B yom
could execute some POSIX scripts.
